>
  <p>You can't put a tape measure on a 150-year-old plaster cornice without risking a piece of it coming off in your hand. Historic fabric is often fragile, irregular, and irreplaceable — which makes the traditional way of measuring a building, by physically touching every surface, exactly the wrong approach. This is the case laser scanning was almost made for.</p>

  <h2>Non-contact is the whole point</h2>

  <p>A laser scanner measures by light. It captures millions of precise points across a surface without anything ever touching it. For a fragile historic structure, that distinction is everything: you get a complete, accurate, three-dimensional record of the building without putting a single hand, ladder, or instrument against material that can't take it.</p>

  <p>That same non-contact capture also handles the geometry that defeats tape measures — the settled floors, the walls that are no longer plumb, the hand-carved ornament that was never straight to begin with. A scanner records what's actually there, irregularities and all, rather than forcing a crooked old building into the clean orthogonal lines a tape measure assumes.</p>

  <h2>The standards that govern this work</h2>

  <p>Historic documentation frequently has to satisfy formal standards, and the deliverables are more demanding than typical commercial work:</p>

  <p><strong>HABS</strong> — the Historic American Buildings Survey, administered by the National Park Service — sets documentation standards for buildings of historic significance. HABS drawings are highly detailed measured drawings, and laser scanning has become a standard way to produce the accurate base geometry they require.</p>

  <p><strong>SHPO</strong> — the State Historic Preservation Office — reviews work affecting historic properties at the state level. Projects seeking approval or historic tax credits often need documentation that meets SHPO expectations.</p>

  <p><strong>The Secretary of the Interior's Standards for the Treatment of Historic Properties</strong> guide preservation, rehabilitation, restoration, and reconstruction work. Accurate existing-conditions documentation underpins compliance with all four treatment approaches.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Accuracy for preservation</div>
    <p>Preservation and restoration frequently justify the tightest accuracy tier — <a href="/resources/ultimate-guide-as-built-drawings/">LOA 50</a> — because the work may involve fabricating replacement elements to match originals, or documenting fine ornament and profiles precisely. This is one of the few existing-conditions contexts where the highest accuracy is genuinely warranted rather than over-specified.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What you can do with the data afterward</h2>

  <p>Beyond satisfying documentation standards, a historic scan produces a dataset with a long useful life:</p>

  <p><strong>A permanent archival record.</strong> The point cloud captures the building exactly as it stands, on a known date. If the structure is later damaged or lost, that record is the reference for any restoration or reconstruction — and an irreplaceable archive in its own right.</p>

  <p><strong>Replacement fabrication.</strong> When ornament, moldings, or structural elements need to be replicated, the scan provides the exact profiles and dimensions to fabricate matching pieces.</p>

  <p><strong>Restoration planning and monitoring.</strong> Scans taken over time can be compared to track movement — settlement, lean, deflection — so conservators can see whether a condition is stable or progressing.</p>

  <p><strong>Interpretation and access.</strong> The same data can drive virtual tours and 3D models that let the public experience a fragile or restricted structure without physical impact on it.</p>

  <h2>How a preservation scan runs on site</h2>

  <p>Non-contact capture is inherently gentle, but historic sites still call for care: coordinating around occupancy or public hours, respecting access restrictions, and capturing the fine detail these projects demand, which usually means denser scanning and more setups than a routine commercial job. The result is a registered point cloud precise enough to derive HABS-quality measured drawings, replacement profiles, or a full BIM model, depending on what the project needs.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>

  <p>Historic buildings are, by definition, irreplaceable — and often too fragile to measure the old way. Laser scanning documents them completely and accurately without laying a finger on them, satisfies the formal standards preservation work has to meet, and leaves behind an archival record that protects the structure long after the project ends. For anyone responsible for a historic property, that combination is hard to beat.</p>
